[SPEAKER_00]: exciting brand. If you haven't heard our episode yet, we have a beautiful in-depth episode with Stefan Steigler on the Paragliding

46:36.595 --> 46:44.342
[SPEAKER_00]: Atlas podcast. Just scroll through the index and you will find all the awesomeness that goes behind this amazing brand that comes out of South

46:44.402 --> 46:54.229
[SPEAKER_00]: Tyrol. Yes, yes. I heard the episode with Stefan. A cool one, yeah. He's a really funny guy, little bit crazy as well with his Ironman stuff, but

46:54.270 --> 47:04.757
[SPEAKER_01]: just a genius in designing wings. Yeah, I was like, that's going to be super cool that a designer is competing in Excel and performing at the

47:04.797 --> 47:12.682
[SPEAKER_00]: highest level and knowing everything that goes behind it. I really hope he smashes the Ironman that he was talking about. But before I move on,

47:12.722 --> 47:24.030
[SPEAKER_00]: Andreas, the type of support varies brand to brand, person to person, project to project. Is getting free wings only for the top elite or can

47:24.410 --> 47:37.982
[SPEAKER_00]: people who have big dreams and the will also look at getting a whole wing sponsored? What's your take on that? So the amount of sponsoring that

47:38.022 --> 47:49.631
[SPEAKER_01]: you get from a company... In my experience, it's mostly connected with the story that you can give them in return. You don't have to be the best

47:49.891 --> 47:58.873
[SPEAKER_01]: cross-country pilot, do the longest flight and so on. You have to make the best story for them. When they give you good conditions for a wing or

47:58.933 --> 48:08.438
[SPEAKER_01]: even a wing for free, they of course they expect some kind of marketing value for them of course this is the deal you are fully convinced about

48:08.478 --> 48:24.108
[SPEAKER_01]: the company about the wing and you just love it and fly it and yeah do at the end marketing for this wing in a very honest way but We see this so often in

48:24.168 --> 48:34.092
[SPEAKER_01]: the topic of climbing. There are the top climbers in the world and they climb the high grades in difficulty in rock climbing. They do

48:34.172 --> 48:43.574
[SPEAKER_01]: presentations as well and they talk about the hardest climb in the audience when you're not in this top league of climbing. You cannot

48:44.134 --> 48:55.718
[SPEAKER_01]: understand the difference between difficulty 10 and 11. It's just everything looks like a vertical wall with no structure. So... It's

48:55.858 --> 49:03.942
[SPEAKER_01]: effort. It makes no sense to explain to an audience the difference between 10 and 11 in climbing. It's the same. It's just unbelievable for

49:03.962 --> 49:11.305
[SPEAKER_01]: them. And so it's with flying. In a good presentation, in a good marketing, in a good story, you don't have to talk about the last 10% in

49:11.385 --> 49:23.969
[SPEAKER_01]: performance, in precision, and so on. How you squeezed out the last maybe 20 kilometers. This is just for the insiders, for the few people

49:24.049 --> 49:33.379
[SPEAKER_01]: who are aware of this, who can understand this. The wider audience, they just want to hear a good story. And it's not about the highest

49:33.439 --> 49:42.421
[SPEAKER_01]: performance. And this is the same for us. We are not professional athletes. We are not in the highest difficulties. We do not fly cross

49:42.481 --> 49:54.385
[SPEAKER_01]: country for 200 kilometers. And we don't do the hardest hike in flies and the most dangerous takeoffs. No, because it's not that interesting for

49:54.405 --> 50:03.143
[SPEAKER_01]: our audience. For audience, the whole story must be a good one. And you don't have to be the best pilot for this. You have to tell a good story. And

50:03.183 --> 50:12.430
[SPEAKER_01]: I think this is the key factor about the good cooperation that we have with our companies. And I think it's maybe also an advice for people who

50:12.470 --> 50:21.397
[SPEAKER_01]: want to get into this. You don't have to just sell your competition results. You have to just sell a good story of you. Maybe you have a cool

50:21.437 --> 50:31.424
[SPEAKER_01]: project starting here because you had the idea for so long and you cross with paraglider from this country to another. Just the story must be

50:31.444 --> 50:44.436
[SPEAKER_01]: cool and interesting. And I think this is, yeah, it's often mistakenly seen as you have to be the top pilot. Only then you get sponsored. No, it's

50:44.497 --> 50:51.624
[SPEAKER_01]: about the good story. You have to touch people's heart, get their interest, and this is with a good story. You can even be a beginner pilot,

50:51.664 --> 51:02.196
[SPEAKER_01]: but if you have a good story, your story is, I begin paragliding because of this. It's way better than you have 10 points more in a competition for

51:02.236 --> 51:10.578
[SPEAKER_01]: the story you might tell in a magazine or a presentation. 100%. And I think this makes so much sense because last episode with Benjamin

51:10.618 --> 51:19.343
[SPEAKER_00]: Kellett, who also happens to be the most followed Instagram cross-country pilot in the sport, he says he emphasizes on the exact

51:19.383 --> 51:30.329
[SPEAKER_00]: same thing. It's like you don't have to go out and have millions of likes or millions of views on your reels. All you have to do is create mutual

51:30.389 --> 51:39.411
[SPEAKER_00]: value for yourself and for the brand and be a good storyteller. The bottom line is that as long as you're able to transfer your energies to

51:39.451 --> 51:48.575
[SPEAKER_00]: your audiences and put a smile on their faces, the brands are more than happy to come along and help you do it. Yeah, I really enjoyed listening

51:48.735 --> 51:59.605
[SPEAKER_01]: to this episode as well, because I had the feeling that he's also taking it not too serious and just do what he likes. And so it reminded me a little

51:59.625 --> 52:10.630
[SPEAKER_01]: bit of how I approach my videos, of course, in a way, lower quality level than he does. But I think the approach could be a little bit the same. Now,

52:10.690 --> 52:19.953
[SPEAKER_00]: glad you found it useful. And I really hope some of the audience members are also in the same boat because subconsciously, you know, we see

52:19.993 --> 52:28.276
[SPEAKER_00]: pilots like Hunter and Hamard or Antion Girard or Tom or Paul or like all of these Red Bull guys who go out there with all these drones and all the

52:28.316 --> 52:36.359
[SPEAKER_00]: fancy gears and smashing distances in places where it's seemingly impossible to even exist, not just for human life, but even for animal

52:36.399 --> 52:43.884
[SPEAKER_00]: life. And we are like, all right, that is one way of living out your dream. But then there are people like you and Benjamin who are like, hey, you

52:43.924 --> 52:51.269
[SPEAKER_00]: don't have to go out and recreate the wheel. You just have to go out and have fun. And there are people willing to make the most out of it as long as

52:51.289 --> 53:01.568
[SPEAKER_00]: there's a will. Yeah, as you mentioned, Antoine Girard, I really like what he's doing, although it's very dangerous stuff. If you know the

53:01.768 --> 53:12.789
[SPEAKER_01]: areas where he takes off and lands on 7,000 meters or almost 7,000 meters, like on Spantik, it's just crazy dangerous, I think. But I think

53:12.829 --> 53:23.932
[SPEAKER_01]: he's just loving what he does and push the limits with this. And I really like what he does. And I always say, It's maybe just a matter of time until

53:24.032 --> 53:33.394
[SPEAKER_01]: somebody will fly into Karakorum, maybe to Broad Peak, land just below the summit, climb an 8,000 meter peak and fly back home in the same day. I

53:33.434 --> 53:41.436
[SPEAKER_01]: think we will see this. And Antoine did the same on Spantik. And this is just amazing. It's the combination of climbing and paragliding what

53:41.536 --> 53:51.945
[SPEAKER_01]: opens up new possibilities. One day you could do an 8,000 meter peaks by paragliding in one day. Wow, this would be just awesome. 20 questions,

53:52.285 --> 53:57.610
[SPEAKER_00]: 20 thoughts are coming to my mind, but I'm just going to stick to one because you're talking about Antoine, the pioneering legend of

53:58.290 --> 54:04.856
[SPEAKER_00]: 8,000ers. Oh my God. I loved him. We had him on the show when I was just starting out and he was super kind. He was like, yeah, man, I'm going to

54:04.896 --> 54:11.943
[SPEAKER_00]: come out on your show and share all my awesomeness with your audiences. When it comes to talking about dangerous, dangers, or while choosing

54:11.963 --> 54:23.485
[SPEAKER_00]: dangers, His lifestyle is scary, exciting, mesmerizing for a lot of us paragliders. But take that same point of view and put it to a layman

54:23.765 --> 54:32.712
[SPEAKER_00]: looking at what we are doing. That layman who's not in paragliding can have the exact same perception for whatever we guys are doing in a normal

54:32.752 --> 54:39.641
[SPEAKER_00]: top to bottom. I remember Antoine spoke about it on the show as well. For any of our listeners who's curious, just scroll down to one of the

54:39.721 --> 54:50.791
[SPEAKER_00]: beginner episodes. You'll see Antoine sharing all his wisdom. He went out and climbed K2 all by himself when he was 17. Can you believe it? zero

54:50.871 --> 54:58.416
[SPEAKER_00]: support solo climb top to bottom he didn't fly down he was not into paragliding and that to me is dangerous and i was like anton what were you

54:58.436 --> 55:06.961
[SPEAKER_00]: thinking i was like no just another day in my life bro that's like nothing out of my comfort zone yeah so the pure essence of it i was like are you

55:07.041 --> 55:18.250
[SPEAKER_00]: kidding me how did you become like that and how can others be there as well And I don't know if you are aware about the story, but it was a Norwegian

55:18.370 --> 55:29.252
[SPEAKER_01]: mountain climber. It was maybe in the 90s. He climbed Mount Everest, but he went to Nepal with his bike, bicycle, drove all the way from Norway to

55:29.352 --> 55:39.576
[SPEAKER_01]: Nepal with all his climbing equipment, climbed Mount Everest, went back down and cycled the whole back to Norway. Unfortunately, he is not

55:39.616 --> 55:49.309
[SPEAKER_01]: so well known, but this is just a story that is amazing. I don't remember the name at the moment, but you know about the story? I've definitely

55:49.349 --> 55:56.133
[SPEAKER_00]: come across that card while scrolling on social media, and it rings a bell. It said Swedish. There was a Swedish guy, but I don't know, could

55:56.154 --> 56:03.879
[SPEAKER_00]: have been Norwegian. One of the Scandinavians who was just too tired of cold and dark and wanted to escape. If you allow me, Andreas, we've been

56:03.919 --> 56:11.624
[SPEAKER_00]: talking about storytelling for quite some time. Storytelling is an art, and any art form gets better the more you create it. There's no

56:11.684 --> 56:18.890
[SPEAKER_00]: rocket science behind it. How do you make the magic in your storytelling? What are the raw materials? What are the hardware? How

56:18.930 --> 56:27.313
[SPEAKER_00]: does the scripting look like? Just tell our audiences a little bit about your thought process behind this. So this is again, very little

56:27.333 --> 56:38.316
[SPEAKER_01]: planning. So almost never a script for it. And we do the tour, the flights, the climb. In the beginning, I have a feeling if it's worth

56:38.456 --> 56:47.679
[SPEAKER_01]: taking more camera equipment with me because it might be good records. Good shots. Sometimes I just take my small camera because I think, no,

56:47.719 --> 56:59.524
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm not in the mood or I don't expect good images. A little bit of thinking in the beginning, but we almost never have a script. We do our climb, our

56:59.564 --> 57:10.510
[SPEAKER_01]: flight. Afterwards, I go through the footage that I have and maybe an idea pops up. If I make a small video out of it or it has potential for a

57:10.570 --> 57:20.336
[SPEAKER_01]: longer video. And maybe this takes a few times. So maybe at the first going through the footage, I have no idea. I say, okay, it's nice, but no

57:20.416 --> 57:29.502
[SPEAKER_01]: idea what to do with it. So maybe I just leave it and see it again a week later or even half a year later. And then I have the idea and say, oh, now I

57:29.522 --> 57:38.245
[SPEAKER_01]: have the time to do a longer video out of it. I always enjoy this process. So I have some footage and just to see what you can make out of it. This is

57:38.285 --> 57:48.829
[SPEAKER_01]: the way that we handle it in most cases. For me, it would be a little bit of boring maybe if I have an exact plan what I shoot and I have all the footage

57:48.849 --> 57:57.350
[SPEAKER_01]: that I need and then I just combine it. I think it would be a little bit of boring for me. So playing with what we have. is a bit more exciting

57:57.390 --> 58:05.499
[SPEAKER_01]: because it opens up, yeah, new possibilities. Maybe it's harder to get the story in the video because you say, I don't have the footage of the

58:05.539 --> 58:13.307
[SPEAKER_01]: approach, but I have to tell it. Then you have to think about different ways to tell this element, or maybe it's a whole different story. Going

58:13.447 --> 58:25.621
[SPEAKER_01]: out, shooting photos and videos and afterwards like juggling with this and make a good story out of it is for me a very exciting part of this also

58:25.661 --> 58:34.008
[SPEAKER_01]: when we are doing live presentations about our climbs it's more or less the same so we do the climbing and maybe one or two years later we have the

58:34.048 --> 58:42.096
[SPEAKER_01]: idea for a live presentation we see ah we did this climb could be a cool topic in the presentation and we did this and so you have to juggle with

58:42.156 --> 58:52.336
[SPEAKER_01]: all the the photos and videos that we have and we do a good presentation out of it. For the videos or for the photos, Whenever I have the mood, then

58:52.396 --> 59:03.800
[SPEAKER_01]: I take a little bit bigger camera, my Sony Alpha 7 III for taking photos, also videos sometimes. The lenses I choose depending on the light

59:03.860 --> 59:14.983
[SPEAKER_01]: conditions or let's say if I expect much landscape, then I take the wide angle. If it's more like a detail story, of course, a more tele lens to get

59:15.043 --> 59:27.238
[SPEAKER_01]: a little bit closer. Of course, we have GoPros. Marlies has one and I have one. So we have the possibility to take video Marlies on her own and I for

59:27.298 --> 59:39.556
[SPEAKER_01]: myself. With two GoPros, I also have the chance to put one on my foot to record me in flight or to put one into the wing. So I have different

59:39.616 --> 59:53.698
[SPEAKER_01]: perspectives. We both have small cameras like a Canon G7X Mark II. These are compact cameras, but they shoot in RAW. And very good video when I'm

59:53.778 --> 01:00:01.260
[SPEAKER_01]: not in the mood of carrying the Sony. I take the small one. A few months ago, I tried the 360 camera because...

01:00:05.814 --> 01:00:15.297
[SPEAKER_01]: When I took GoPro videos of paragliding, I always had the problem that, yeah, I thought this would be a good angle and hassle with all the camera

01:00:15.337 --> 01:00:24.059
[SPEAKER_01]: equipment, do the flight. And in the end you see the footage and you say, okay, but just half of it you can use because you took the wrong angle and

01:00:24.079 --> 01:00:33.942
[SPEAKER_01]: so on. So I said, maybe a 360 camera could help me. And so I'm just trying to figure out how to use this in the best way.

01:00:36.840 --> 01:00:43.486
[SPEAKER_00]: 360s are so addictive with post-processing because they make your life easier. You're never going to go back to GoPro the moment you switch. So

01:00:43.506 --> 01:00:51.733
[SPEAKER_00]: just letting you know, this is what happened with me. I got a 360 and my GoPros are just kicked. For me right at the moment, it's a little bit of

01:00:51.853 --> 01:01:00.460
[SPEAKER_01]: more hurry and more hustle in the post-production because I'm not into editing 360 videos with defining the camera angle. I'm not so used to it

01:01:00.520 --> 01:01:09.684
[SPEAKER_01]: yet, but it's a lot of fun. One perfect advantage is you can make a landscape and portrait format from the same footage. Nowadays, if you

01:01:09.724 --> 01:01:16.910
[SPEAKER_01]: want to take the video for YouTube, you have landscape and for Instagram Reels, it's better to have a portrait. You just have one footage and

01:01:16.930 --> 01:01:27.398
[SPEAKER_01]: decide afterwards. It's a very big advantage. Yes. And I have a little drone, the Mavic Mini 2. And when I'm in the mood of carrying camera

01:01:27.438 --> 01:01:37.717
[SPEAKER_01]: equipment, I also take this one with me. Yeah. Nice. I have to tell it to the audiences. You make it sound super simple. The way you're saying

01:01:37.737 --> 01:01:44.280
[SPEAKER_00]: that you just go out with the flow and create and somehow it turns out beautiful. I was speaking to a video editor that I'm planning to work

01:01:44.320 --> 01:01:52.564
[SPEAKER_00]: with because we are also planning to come out with videos on paragliding atlas. And somehow, Jan-Baptiste Chandelier, I hope I'm saying his

01:01:52.604 --> 01:02:03.199
[SPEAKER_00]: name correctly. Man, the cinematic brilliance that guy brings to our screen out of flight. Oh my God. How does he do it? I know he has a

01:02:03.239 --> 01:02:11.565
[SPEAKER_00]: masterclass, which is all in French. And he does divulge few details. What would it take to do that? And I sent that video to my editor. And I was

01:02:11.585 --> 01:02:18.390
[SPEAKER_00]: like, what do I have to do? He's like, bro, forget it. You don't have the money. You don't have the team. You don't have the whole

01:02:18.470 --> 01:02:26.833
[SPEAKER_00]: infrastructure. But what I'm trying to say is that sometimes it takes one hour for a 10-second shot. to be taken at that quality. There are

01:02:26.933 --> 01:02:35.479
[SPEAKER_00]: easier ways to do it. And then there are endless ways to make it better, which is you have to draw the line that where do I stop? As you mentioned

01:02:35.559 --> 01:02:47.145
[SPEAKER_01]: earlier, climbing is maybe a little bit more popular right now. And paragliding is not. If you mention Jean-Baptiste with his videos, this

01:02:47.185 --> 01:02:58.821
[SPEAKER_01]: is the whole new world that he opened. And I also really enjoyed watching the videos from him and compared to climbing. I cannot think about any

01:02:59.001 --> 01:03:08.946
[SPEAKER_01]: video in climbing like this, just to have this happiness in filming perspectives and just the ideas that he makes. So I can't think of a

01:03:09.006 --> 01:03:18.335
[SPEAKER_01]: creative video like this in the climbing context. So this is what paragliding has in advantage here. with a few guys like this. So if there

01:03:18.415 --> 01:03:25.026
[SPEAKER_00]: are people out there who have been thinking about when to get started, now is the time. Pull up your socks and go out because the world is

01:03:25.066 --> 01:03:36.532
[SPEAKER_00]: responding to anyone who's trying. Simple as that. And I think I would like to add here as well, just as with sponsorship and being an extreme

01:03:36.972 --> 01:03:44.955
[SPEAKER_01]: athlete or something, you don't need the best equipment to shoot a good story. All the mobile phones are usually good enough. If you focus on a

01:03:44.995 --> 01:03:53.757
[SPEAKER_01]: few things, the quality is fully okay to make a good story, a good video. Many of the videos in our presentation about climbing Manaslu in

01:03:53.797 --> 01:04:03.748
[SPEAKER_01]: Himalaya, I took with my iPhone. And I'm always surprised when I'm on stage and I look at the videos in five by eight meters. I think, hey, this

01:04:03.789 --> 01:04:12.965
[SPEAKER_01]: was shot by an iPhone and it's fully okay. It depends on the story to tell with it. And even it could be beneficial if the quality is not that good

01:04:13.005 --> 01:04:21.989
[SPEAKER_01]: because it makes it more honest. It fits better to your story. If you have a high quality image and footage, maybe it doesn't connect good with the

01:04:22.009 --> 01:04:28.912
[SPEAKER_01]: story that you tell about. Having a rough time and getting good shots is meh. There's something. Andreas, once you get into this game, because

01:04:28.972 --> 01:04:38.521
[SPEAKER_00]: it's so addictive and rewarding and you start winning at life as you finally plan to, Does your flying only revolve around projects or do you

01:04:38.582 --> 01:04:50.205
[SPEAKER_00]: still find time that there are days that today it's just me and my wing and nobody else either in my head or physically? Yes, I still have flights

01:04:50.285 --> 01:04:58.732
[SPEAKER_01]: like this. This is also one part that we enjoy here with the house that we bought here. We are in the area that we lived before, so we came back more

01:04:58.792 --> 01:05:08.619
[SPEAKER_01]: or less. And of course, we know all the mountains here, the takeoffs. And we have one very nice little mountain. It's a good takeoff. You can take

01:05:08.700 --> 01:05:20.408
[SPEAKER_01]: off in three directions. It's from the door. It's maybe two hours of walk and I can fly almost until back to my house door. This is still something

01:05:20.448 --> 01:05:32.311
[SPEAKER_01]: that we really enjoy if we just take two hours or two and a half hours of time out and walk up there. Even if we walked up this way for maybe 500

01:05:33.032 --> 01:05:42.354
[SPEAKER_01]: times an hour, it's never getting boring because we just like the landscape, the view, the difference in spring, in summer, looking at

01:05:42.395 --> 01:05:53.341
[SPEAKER_01]: the trees and then just sitting at the takeoff. a few minutes and enjoying the landscape of our hometown here. Yeah, we still enjoy this.

01:05:53.681 --> 01:06:04.390
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah. And I don't have to take a camera with me on that. Andreas, $8,000, man. This is a magic number in mountaineering. Paragliding is catching

01:06:04.450 --> 01:06:11.956
[SPEAKER_00]: up very quick. What does it take to, first of all, dream about it, and secondly, make it happen?

01:06:15.279 --> 01:06:15.399
[SPEAKER_01]: Uh...

01:06:18.650 --> 01:06:28.372
[SPEAKER_01]: You really need a dream because it costs a lot. It's a lot of risk involved. And if you're not completely passionate about this, I think

01:06:28.412 --> 01:06:43.255
[SPEAKER_01]: you want to do it. It's just too much risk. But since I dreamed of this, it was just logical that I would like to try this on my own one time. So you

01:06:43.295 --> 01:06:52.255
[SPEAKER_01]: need really a good dream about this. Did you really do it? I think for me personally, this was the most important thing. Just out of curiosity,

01:06:52.295 --> 01:07:01.943
[SPEAKER_00]: Andreas, to tell our listeners a little bit about how does life look like at the takeoff of a 8000er? What are the emotions? What are the

01:07:02.143 --> 01:07:11.630
[SPEAKER_00]: conditions? How is the takeoff run? How does the wind come up? So I cannot tell you about the takeoff on 8000 meter. My highest takeoff was in

01:07:11.650 --> 01:07:21.986
[SPEAKER_01]: Pakistan at Gondwara Holadis was about 6000 meters. Hmm. On some days, even on 8,000 meters, you would have little wind. So the chances to take

01:07:22.147 --> 01:07:32.173
[SPEAKER_01]: off, you have them, but you have to be at the right place at the right time. As the guys on K2 were, this was just amazing. And I think it was four of

01:07:32.213 --> 01:07:43.961
[SPEAKER_01]: them flying down. So the chance is there. From my flight, from the Gondogorula pass, during the trekking back in the Karakorum, you cross

01:07:44.001 --> 01:07:45.482
[SPEAKER_01]: this pass. It's about 6,000 meters, I think.

01:07:48.630 --> 01:07:57.922
[SPEAKER_01]: It was a funny story because we were going back and some porters were going with us. Each day they told us, it's about five hours walk today.

01:07:58.103 --> 01:08:07.928
[SPEAKER_01]: And for us, it was always like seven hours because they were just so fast and we were so exhausted. And then there was this one day where we should

01:08:07.988 --> 01:08:17.391
[SPEAKER_01]: cross this pass and they said, ah, we have to wake up very early. It's a long day up there. We start at maybe 2 AM. And so we are at the pass at

01:08:17.631 --> 01:08:25.014
[SPEAKER_01]: sunrise. We had no problem. So we were hiking up there in the morning and it was feeling good. And I thought, ah, I think we are reaching the top of

01:08:25.054 --> 01:08:36.973
[SPEAKER_01]: the pass, but it's still far away from sunrise. So we were two hours before sunrise on the pass. It was very little wind, a perfect takeoff. I

01:08:36.993 --> 01:08:46.554
[SPEAKER_01]: said, okay, we're just too early, but I would like to wait here until it gets light and I can fly down. We were waiting there for two hours.

01:08:46.874 --> 01:08:54.259
[SPEAKER_01]: Unfortunately, Marlies got too cold and she decided that she don't want to take all the hassle with taking out the wing in the cold and then

01:08:54.319 --> 01:09:03.786
[SPEAKER_01]: packing it again. So she took the normal way down. It just takes two hours walking down. So it's not about saving you much time of the hike. It's

01:09:03.826 --> 01:09:14.313
[SPEAKER_01]: just about doing this flight. I was shivering cold up there, but then sun came up and it was light enough so that I could take off. And it was very

01:09:14.354 --> 01:09:27.321
[SPEAKER_01]: flat. I was really a little bit surprised on how fast you have to run until the wing carries your weight. So this was a little bit unexpected. But in

01:09:27.361 --> 01:09:36.326
[SPEAKER_01]: the end, I was flying, just looking back and seeing Broad Peak, K2, and in front of me, Lila Peak. It was just amazing. So it was fully worth waiting

01:09:36.366 --> 01:09:43.645
[SPEAKER_01]: for two hours in the cold night until sunlight came. I can imagine. Bragging rights for life. Yeah.

01:09:47.785 --> 01:09:54.826
[SPEAKER_00]: Just out of curiosity, you must be using glacier shoes, right? Like the ones which aren't flexible and they have to have some sort of crampons or

01:09:54.846 --> 01:10:04.068
